== Husbands and the Love of Christ

=== §. 26. #emph[Of the sum of Husbands duties.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:25.]

    #text(style: "italic")[25\. Husbands, love your#super[+] wives, just as Christ loved the church and gave Himself up for her.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[From] the duties of wives the apostle goes on to press the duties of husbands. And as he set before wives the example of the church as a pattern, so he sets before husbands the example of Christ; and #footnote[#emph[v.] 28, 29.] he adds to it the pattern of a man’s own self, in regard of that natural affection which he bears to his body. So he adds pattern to pattern, #footnote[Husbands’ duties most pressed.] and he presses husbands’ duties the more largely and earnestly, because husbands have a more honorable place, and so their failing in duty is the more heinous, scandalous, and dangerous.

The apostle limits the duties of husbands to their #footnote[ἑαυτῶν.] #emph[own] wives, as he did the duties of wives to their #footnote[ἰδίοις.] #emph[own] husbands. For though the same word is not used here as was used before, yet a word of like emphasis is used; and there is as good reason that our English translators should have put this particle #emph[(own)] into this verse as into the 22nd verse. For proof of this, read 1 Corinthians 7:2, where #footnote[ἑαυτοῦ. ἴδιον. See §. 82.] these two words are used, and both of them are translated #emph[own.]

I have noted this all the more because many who hold that a wife must have only one husband imagine that a husband may have more wives than one; and this particle #emph[(own)] wipes that notion away. All the duties of a husband are comprised under this one word #footnote[See the reasons for this in #emph[Treatise] 4, §. 2.] #emph[love.] So that a husband might be the better directed in this love, and the more stirred up to it, the example of Christ named before, and of his love to the church, is set forth very vividly: first in general in these words, “just as Christ loved the church,” and then more particularly in the words that follow.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of the example of Christ’s love.]

The note of comparison#footnote[καθώς.] (#emph[just as)] requires no #emph[equality,] as if it were possible for a husband to love his wife in the same #emph[measure] as Christ loved his church (for as Christ exceeds man in excellence and greatness, so he does in love and tenderness). But it notes an #emph[equity,] and a #emph[like quality.]

An #emph[equity,] because there is as great reason that husbands, by virtue of their place, should love their wives, as that Christ, by virtue of his place, should love the church.

A #emph[like quality,] because the love which Christ bears to the church is in every way without exception, and it is a love which turns to the good and benefit of the church. From this, note two points.

1\. #emph[Husbands must come as near as they can to Christ in loving their wives.] In this respect, because they can never love as much as Christ did, they must never think they have loved enough.

2\. Though their love cannot equal Christ’s love in #emph[measure,] yet in its #emph[manner] it must be like Christ’s: #emph[a love that goes before, a true, free, pure, exceeding, constant love.]

#emph[The] measure #emph[and] manner #emph[of Christ’s love are set out distinctly in] Treatise 4, §. 61, #emph[etc., and the love which a husband owes his wife is there compared with it and applied to it. The same application may also be made of that mutual Christian love which we owe one another.]

The love of Christ to the church is amplified:

+ By an #emph[effect] of it, in these words: “gave Himself up for her.”
+ By the #emph[end] of that effect, set down at length in v. 26, 27.

The #emph[effect] is noted partly to confirm the truth, partly to declare the measure of Christ’s love.

The #emph[act (he gave)] shows that his love was in deed and in truth, not only in show and pretense.

The #emph[object (himself)] shows that he loved his church more than his own life. A greater evidence of love could not be given, for “greater love has no one than this, that he #footnote[John 15:13.] lay down his life for his friends.”

The #emph[end] of Christ’s love (set forth in v. 26, 27) is noted to show that he loved his church so for her good and happiness, rather than for any advantage to himself.

As this example of Christ’s love to his church is set before husbands, so it may and ought also to be applied to all Christians, and that in a double respect.

1\. As a motive to stir them up to love both Christ himself and also their brethren.

2\. As a pattern to teach them how to love.

It is a motive to love Christ, because love deserves love, especially such a love, from such a person, as the love of Christ is. Indeed, our love of Christ is an evidence that we are loved by Christ, as smoke is a sign of fire. Therefore, both in thankfulness to Christ for his love to us, and for the assurance of our own souls of Christ’s love to us, we ought in all things that we can to testify our love to Christ.

It is also a motive to love our brethren, because, Christ being in heaven, “our goodness does not extend to him”;#footnote[Ps 16:2.] but our brethren on earth stand in his place, and the love we show to them we show to him, and he accepts it as done to him. “You#super[+] fed Me, you visited Me,”#footnote[Matt 25:35, 40.] says Christ to those who fed and visited his brethren. This love too, the #footnote[1 John 4:20.] love of our brethren, is an evidence that we are loved by God. Therefore, “if Christ so loved us, we also ought to love one another.”#footnote[#emph[and v.] 11.]

How the love of Christ is a pattern I will #footnote[Treatise 4, §. 61, #emph[etc.]] show later.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of Christ’s giving himself.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:25.]

    #text(style: "italic")[And gave Himself up for her.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[This] fruit and effect of Christ’s love extends to all #footnote[Christ himself, and all that he did and suffered, are ours.] the things that Christ did or suffered for our redemption: that he came down from heaven, took our nature on himself, and became a man; that he subjected himself to the law, and perfectly fulfilled it; that he made himself subject to many temptations of the devil and his instruments; that he took our infirmities on himself; that he became a King to govern us, a Prophet to instruct us, a Priest to make atonement for us; that he subjected himself to death, the cursed death of the cross, and so made himself an oblation and sacrifice for our sins; that he was buried; that he rose again; that he ascended into heaven, and sits there at God’s right hand to make intercession for us. For after Christ had taken it on himself to be our #emph[head] and #emph[Savior,] he set himself apart wholly for our use and our benefit. So his person, his offices, his actions, his sufferings, his humiliation, his exaltation, the dignity, the purity, the efficacy of all of them, belong to the church, and they all tend to her good. This in general is the extent of this fruit of Christ’s love: he “gave Himself up for her.”

More particularly, we may note these three points:

+ The #emph[action,] what he did #emph[(he gave.)]
+ The #emph[object,] what he gave #emph[(himself.)]
+ The #emph[end,] why he gave himself #emph[(for her),] for the church’s good.

The #emph[action,] having relation to the #emph[object,] points most especially to the death of Christ. The #footnote[παρέδωκεν.] Greek word is a compound word, and signifies to #emph[give up.] It implies two things:

1\. #emph[That Christ died willingly:] the simple word #emph[(gave)] indicates that much.

2\. #emph[That his death was an oblation,] that is, a price of redemption, or a satisfaction: the compound word #emph[(gave up)] indicates that much.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of the willingness of Christ to die.]

That Christ died willingly is evident from the circumstances noted about his death. When Peter counseled him to spare himself, and not to go to Jerusalem (where he was to be put to death), #footnote[Matt 16:22, 23.] he called him Satan, and said that he was “a stumbling block” to him. When Judas went out to betray him, #footnote[John 13:27.] he said to him, “What you are about to do, do quickly.” #footnote[John 18:2. #emph[etc. Audiui, Domine, auditum tuum et expavi, dum quaeris à quaerentibus quid quaerant, cùm noueris quod te quaerant: & dixisti te esse Iesum Nazarenum. Cypr. de Pass. Christi.] \[I have heard your report, Lord, and was afraid, when you ask those who seek you what they seek, though you knew that they were seeking you; and you said that you were Jesus of Nazareth.\]] When Judas had gone out to get a company to arrest him, he went to the place where he usually went, so that Judas might readily find him. Indeed, he went halfway to meet those who came to take him, and he asked them whom they were seeking, though he knew whom they sought. And when they said, “Jesus of Nazareth,” he answered, “I am He.” When they came to him, he drove them all backward with a word of his mouth, and yet he would not escape from them. #footnote[Matt 26:53.] He could have prayed to the Father to have more than twelve legions of angels to guard him against those who arrested him, but he would not. #footnote[Matt 27:42.] When his adversaries provoked him to come down from the cross, though he could have done so, he would not. #footnote[Mark 15:39.] At the very instant of giving up his spirit he cried with a loud voice, which shows that his life was not then spent: he might have kept it longer if he had wished. And from this the centurion gathered that he was the Son of God. #footnote[John 2:19. Matt 28:6.] When he was actually dead, and laid in a grave, he rose again. These and other such circumstances verify what Christ said of his own life: “No one takes it from Me, but I lay it down of My own accord.”#footnote[John 10:18. #emph[Indicia dedit nulla se necessitate, sed obedientia vrgeri ad mortem. Cypr. de coen. Dom.] \[He gave signs that he was driven to death by no necessity, but by obedience.\] #emph[Reason.]] It was therefore no necessity that compelled him to die, but his voluntary obedience.

Christ is #footnote[Acts 3:15 ἀρχηγὸς τῆς ζωῆς.] #emph[the Lord, Prince] and #emph[Author of life,] and he has absolute power over his own life, as over the life of others.

So then we see that his sacrifice was a voluntary and free gift: its cause was his own will and good pleasure.

This greatly commends the love of Christ, and assures us that his sacrifice is all the more acceptable to God, who “loves a cheerful giver.” #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]]

Let us, in imitation of our head, do the things to which #footnote[2 Cor 9:7.] we are called willingly and cheerfully, however #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] disgraceful they may seem to the world, or grievous to our weak flesh.

=== §. 30. #emph[Of the kind of Christ’s death, an oblation.]

That #emph[Christ’s death was an oblation,] and a price of redemption, is evident from the death of the beasts which were offered up as sacrifices, and in that were a type of Christ’s death. But this apostle notes it expressly where he says that Christ “gave Himself up for us as a fragrant sacrificial offering to God,”#footnote[Eph 5:2.] and again, that Christ “gave Himself as a ransom.”#footnote[1 Tim 2:6.] The phrases #emph[redeeming,]#footnote[1 Pet 1:18, 19] #emph[purchasing,]#footnote[Acts 20:28.] #emph[buying,]#footnote[2 Pet 2:1.] and the like, attributed to Christ and his blood, confirm the same further.

Learn by this to consider Christ’s death, not as the death of #footnote[Use.] a private man, but of a public person, of a surety, of a pledge, who in our place and stead was made “to be sin”#footnote[2 Cor 5:21.] and became “a curse”#footnote[Gal 3:13.] to redeem us from our sins, and from the curse which had fallen on us through sin. The comfort and benefit of Christ’s death is lost if this is not known and believed. In this lies a main difference between the death of Christ and that of all other men, not excepting the most righteous martyrs. Their death was only a duty and a debt: no oblation making satisfaction, no price, no ransom, as Christ’s was.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of the infinite value of the price of our redemption.]

The object, the thing which Christ gave as a ransom, was #emph[himself:] not his body alone, nor his body and soul only, but his person, consisting of his two natures, human and divine.

#emph[Question.] How could his divine nature be given up? Could it #footnote[How the person of Christ, being God-man, was given for us.] suffer? Could it die?

#emph[Answer.] 1. The Deity, considered simply in and by itself, could not die; but that person who was God both could die and did die. For the Son of God took a human nature into the unity of his divine nature, and united them together #footnote[ἀσυγχύτως. ἀτρέπτως. ἀδιαιρέτως. ἀχωρίστως. #emph[Symbol. Calced.]] #emph[without confusion, alteration, division, separation,] in one person. So what is done by one nature is done by the person, and in that respect the Scripture often attributes it to the other nature: as where it is said that they “crucified the Lord of glory,”#footnote[1 Cor 2:8.] and that God “purchased” the church “with His own blood.”#footnote[Acts 20:28.]

2\. Though the divine nature of Christ did not suffer, yet it upheld the human nature, and added dignity, worth and efficacy to the sufferings of that nature.

3\. Christ’s divine nature had its own proper and peculiar works in the work of redemption, such as to sanctify his human nature, to take away our sins, to reconcile us to God, and the like.

So then, in three respects the whole person of Christ was given to us.

1\. In regard of the inseparable union of both natures.

2\. In regard of the assistance of the Deity in the things which the human nature of Christ did.

3\. In regard of some proper actions belonging to the Deity. #footnote[Doctrine.]

Since the person of Christ, God-man, was given up,#footnote[1 Pet 1:19. #emph[Uses.]] I gather that

#emph[The price of our redemption is of infinite value.] Neither Christ #footnote[#emph[Quanta se fiducia spes credentium consolatur considerans quantus quanta pro nondum credentibus passus sit? Aug. de Doctr. Chr. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 15. \[With what confidence does the hope of believers comfort itself, considering how great a one suffered such great things for those who did not yet believe?\]] nor God himself could give a greater. Heaven and earth and all things in them are not of like worth. Well, therefore, might Saint Peter call it “precious blood,” and prefer it before silver, gold, and all other things of price.

1\. What place can be left for despair in those who know and believe the worth of this ransom?

2\. What can be held too dear for him who, notwithstanding the infinite excellence of his person, gave himself for us? Can goods, can friends, can children, can liberty, can life, can anything else?

3\. What just cause do we have to give ourselves up as “living sacrifices, #footnote[Rom 12:1.] holy and pleasing” to him who gave himself for us?

4\. How ungrateful, how unworthy of Christ are those who for his sake will not forsake their unstable honors, fading wealth, vain pleasures, gaudy clothes, and such trash?

=== §. 32. #emph[Of Christ’s seeking the good of the Church.]

The #emph[end] for which Christ gave himself was #emph[for the church, so] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[that Christ in his death aimed at our good.] #footnote[2 Cor 5:21.] He was made “sin for us, so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God”; #footnote[Gal 3:13.] he became “a curse for us,” and “redeemed us from the curse of the law”; #footnote[Gal 1:4.] he gave himself “for our sins to rescue us”; #footnote[John 10:15.] he laid down his life “for the sheep.”

This proves Christ’s giving of himself to be a fruit of his love, for “love does not seek its own.”#footnote[1 Cor 13:5. 1. #emph[Use.]]

Let us learn by this to apply to ourselves all that Christ did. If he gave himself #emph[for us,] then he, and #footnote[See §. 28. 2. #emph[Use.]] all that belongs to him, is ours.

Let us also learn by this how to show love: namely, by seeking and procuring the good of others. “No one should seek his #footnote[1 Cor 10:24.] own good, but the good of others.” If this were practiced, would there be such oppressing, such undermining, such deceiving, such wronging of one another as there is? The apostle’s complaint is proved all too true in our days: “All seek #footnote[Phil 2:21.] their own.” But let that mind be in us which was in Christ Jesus, and let us show our love in this way, as we desire to share in this fruit of Christ’s love.

From this it follows by just consequence that #emph[Christ did not merit] #footnote[Christ did not merit for himself.] #emph[for himself.] Was there any need for Christ to come down from heaven to earth to purchase anything for himself? When he was going out of the world, he prayed thus: “And now, Father, glorify Me in Your presence with the glory I had #footnote[John 17:5.] with You before the world existed.” Did Christ, by anything which he did on earth, merit that glory which he had before the world existed? All the exaltation to which he was advanced, even in his human nature, was due to the dignity of his person.

1\. #emph[Objection.] He endured the cross “for the joy set #footnote[Heb 12:2.] before Him.”

#emph[Answer.] He used that joy, which was due to him by right, as a help to support him in the weakness of his human nature, not as a recompense which he was to earn.

2\. #emph[Objection.] “He … became obedient to death, even death on a cross. #footnote[Phil 2:9 διό.] THEREFORE God exalted Him to the highest place.”

#emph[Answer.] That particle #emph[(therefore)] does not declare the cause, but the order of his exaltation, noting a consequence that followed after his death: after he had humbled himself so low, he was most highly advanced.

3\. #emph[Objection.] Christ, being man, was bound to the law, and therefore he ought to fulfill it for himself.

#emph[Answer.] If he had been merely a man, that would be true. But he united his human nature to his divine, and made one person of both, and that person was God as well as man; so he was bound to nothing further than what it pleased him to subject himself to voluntarily for our sakes.

2\. If Christ had been bound to the law, he must have fulfilled it as a duty; and if he was to fulfill it as a duty, how could he merit by it so high a degree of honor as he is advanced to?

This notion of Christ’s meriting for himself greatly lessens the glory of Christ’s grace and goodness in giving himself.

=== §. 33. #emph[Of the particular ends, why Christ gave himself, and of the condition of the Church before Christ took her.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:26.]

    #text(style: "italic")[That He might sanctify her and cleanse her with the washing of water by the word.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] general #emph[end] of Christ’s giving himself, intimated before in this phrase #emph[(for us),] is set out particularly in this verse and the next, and that in two branches.

One concerns the state of the church in this world, #emph[v.] 26.

The other concerns her state in the world to come, #emph[v.] 27.

The latter of these two is the chief.

The former is subordinate to the latter, an end for accomplishing the other end: for the church is made pure here so that hereafter it may be made glorious.

In laying down the former, he notes

+ The #emph[end] at which Christ aimed.
+ The #emph[means] by which he effected what he aimed at.

That #emph[end] is set forth in these words, “that He might sanctify her, #footnote[καθαρίσας. See §. 39.] having cleansed her” (so they may be translated word for word), so that what stands in the latter place in the order of the words stands in the first place in the order of the matter.

The word #emph[(cleansing)] points to our #emph[justification.]

The word #emph[(sanctifying)] expresses our #emph[sanctification.]

The means of effecting these are two.

+ #emph[Baptism,] comprised under this phrase, #emph[washing of water.]
+ The #emph[word.]

The two branches of the former #emph[end,] namely #emph[cleansing] and #emph[sanctifying,] imply two things in general.

+ The #emph[condition] of the church in itself.
+ The #emph[alteration] of it by Christ.

The #emph[condition] is presupposed, which is that she was impure, polluted, in the common state of corrupt man. Things pure in themselves are not cleansed, but things foul and impure; persons who are of themselves freed and exempted from a common misery do not need another’s help to free and exempt them. Seeing, then, that the church stood in need of being cleansed and sanctified, surely

#emph[The church in herself was, like the world, polluted.] This is set forth very vividly #footnote[Doctrine.] by the prophet Ezekiel under the picture of #emph[a] #footnote[The church in herself polluted.] #emph[wretched infant born of a cursed parentage, whose navel cord was not cut, who was not washed, salted, or swaddled, but cast out into the open field, polluted with blood.]#footnote[Ezek 16:3. #emph[etc.]] The apostle, when he sets forth the wretched state of the world, often notes of the true members #footnote[Eph 2:3.] of the church that “we too” were such.#footnote[Titus 3:3.] #footnote[1 Cor 6:11.]

The church consists of none other than those who came out of Adam’s loins. Now as all the brood that comes from vipers, adders, toads, spiders, and other such venomous mothers is infected with poison, so all the sons of Adam are polluted with sin. “That which is born of the flesh” (as every mother’s child is, not excepting the members of the church, for they have fathers and mothers of their flesh) “is flesh,” that is, polluted and corrupt. Therefore, when we are taken into the church, we are “born again.” #footnote[John 3:3, 5.]

This, our former state by nature, is to be thought of often and seriously, #footnote[Our natural condition to be thought of often.] and that in respect of

- Christ.
- Ourselves.
- Others.

1\. In regard of Christ, to magnify his love all the more. Our former state, before he cast the wings of his mercy over us, shows our unworthiness, our vileness, and our wretchedness; and in that respect it opens our heart and mouth to think and say, “O #smallcaps[Lord], our Lord, … what is man that You are mindful of him, or the son of man that You care for him?”#footnote[Ps 8:1, 4.] “Lord, why are You going to reveal Yourself to us and not to the world?”#footnote[John 14:22.] The right knowledge of our former state, and a due consideration of it, makes us ascribe all the glory of our present dignity and happiness to Christ, who changed our state, as Saint Paul does: “I thank Christ Jesus our Lord, who has strengthened me … I was formerly a blasphemer,” etc.#footnote[1 Tim 1:12. #emph[etc.]] Indeed, it makes us prize and value our present state all the more, as David did.#footnote[2 Sam 7:18, #emph[etc.]]

2\. In regard of ourselves this is to be thought of, to humble us, and to keep us from insolent boasting in those privileges which we share through Christ. To this purpose the apostle presses this point thus: “For who makes #footnote[1 Cor 4:7.] you different from anyone else? What do you have that you did not receive? And if you did receive it, why do you boast as though you did not?” When a man is raised from a lowly place to a great one, and grows proud and insolent because of it, we say, #emph[he has forgotten where he came from.] So the remembrance of our former condition is a means to preserve humility and to suppress insolence.

3\. In regard of others it is to be thought of, to move us to pity all the more the woeful state of those who still remain as we once were; to hope that their state may be changed as well as ours was; and to pray, and to use what means we can, that it may be changed. To stir Christians up to show all meekness to those who were outside, the apostle gives this reason: “For at one time we too were foolish,” #footnote[Titus 3:3, #emph[etc.]] etc. Read how forcibly this is urged in Rom 11:18, 19, etc.
